TOUCHED: src/components/three/HeroScene.tsx, src/components/three/SilkForm.tsx

I created the WebGL centerpiece for Yabhasyuka's quiet luxury spa aesthetic by implementing [HeroScene.tsx](file:///Users/nathan/Documents/code/Yabhasyuka/yabhasyuka-client/.claude/worktrees/website-template/src/components/three/HeroScene.tsx) and [SilkForm.tsx](file:///Users/nathan/Documents/code/Yabhasyuka/yabhasyuka-client/.claude/worktrees/website-template/src/components/three/SilkForm.tsx). `HeroScene` renders a responsive, transparent React Three Fiber `<Canvas>` configured with soft studio lighting (warm key light `#f5e9da`, ambient `#efe7db`, and clay rim `#b08968`), while safely detecting WebGL availability and `prefers-reduced-motion` in a hydration-safe `useEffect` state that renders `null` during SSR or fallback scenarios. Inside the scene, `SilkForm` renders an abstract, organic sculpture using a high-detail `<icosahedronGeometry args={[1.6, 64]} />` and a custom GLSL `ShaderMaterial` assigned directly via `<mesh material={...}>` to avoid JSX namespace hacks. The vertex shader features inline 3D simplex noise for gentle breathing displacement (~0.35 at `uTime * 0.08`) and calculates exact surface normals via finite differences so lighting dynamically catches every fold. The fragment shader blends between clay (`#b08968`) and bone (`#efe7db`) driven by view-space normals, adding a soft porcelain/silk sheen via Fresnel rim-lightening and subtle dither/grain to eliminate color banding. Finally, `useFrame` drives fluid motion with zero per-frame allocations by mutating ref-stored uniforms directly: applying constant slow rotation, pointer parallax easing toward cursor tilt up to ~0.12 radians, and a scroll-recede transition that smoothly elevates, scales down, and fades opacity to zero across the first viewport height.
